Done. Blind round 63 is complete, in three commits in the order the ritual requires.

The round. My instrument picked the target, not me — src/format/tools.rs led yoyo risk epistemic at score 1.7, 203 snapshots unobserved. Three bets committed before I opened the file, graded before I touched any code, mirrored to issue #738.

Score: 0 hit / 2 partial / 1 miss. Both partials failed the same way, which is the finding about me:

• h1 (miss) — I bet ThinkBlockFilter had no end-of-stream reset with a caller. flush() exists, is called at prompt.rs:685, and PromptEventState builds a fresh filter per prompt, so the leak is structurally impossible. That was the genre prior doing exactly what my archive says it does: predicting a defect in code that already learned the lesson.
• h2 (partial) — found the real defect, named the wrong twin. I bet Spinner's teardown was forgotten while its paint path was fixed. Backwards: `Spinner::stop/drop were already correct, and **ToolProgressTimer::stop/drop gated the erase on stderr_is_terminal() alone** — so --screen-reader` printed its one linear line and then wrote \r\x1b[K into a screen reader's stream.
• h3 (partial) — verified its absence exactly as its evidence command promised (zero `is_quiet matches), then slid from "absent" to "wrong" without checking scope. --quiet` documents itself as suppressing informational messages; a self-erasing animation isn't one.

The fix. Not a second patch — the shared rule. All four teardown sites now route through write_teardown_clear over the pure, table-tested `teardown_clear_sequence(is_terminal, plain), and animations_enabled` is gone because two of those four were its only callers. Non-plain users are byte-identical; only the plain branch changes. Pinned at the emission point, since the helper's return value is the string the caller writes.

Two things worth naming honestly. First, CLAUDE.md promised "no \r, no ANSI cursor escapes" for that exact path — the doc line was the assertion the code was breaking, and the existing plain-output test covered the three strings we print and never the string we exit with. Second, my own "no definition without its consumer" rule caught me mid-edit: I added the helper before its call sites and clippy failed instantly. The rule works; I still needed it.

No self-model credit claimed for this one. The find came from holding two sibling teardown paths side by side — that's patience, not self-knowledge.
